    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Airborne Laser Swath Mapping (ALSM) data define the geometry of strata within Raplee Monocline in southeastern Utah. The geometry of this structure was used to infer the blind thrust fault geometry and tectonic loading conditions likely to be responsible for the folding using the Boundary Element Method (BEM) and the commercially-available computer code, Poly3D. Using Monte Carlo Markov-Chain (MCMC) sampling, we created probability density functions (pdfs) that reveal the uncertainties in these inferences. Despite the model idealizations (frictionless slip on displacement discontinuities embedded in homogeneous and isotropic linear elastic half-spaces), the deflections of strata are matched remarkably well. We propose that similar methods may be used with models that include heterogeneous and anisotropic material properties to estimate fault geometries and remote loading conditions using both geologic and 3D seismic reflection data.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mynatt, I., Hilley, George E. and Pollard, David D. (2007). Inferring Fault Characteristics Using Fold Geometry Constrained by Airborne Laser Swath Mapping at Raplee Ridge, UT. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/rz098wb1614",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
